<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaanxi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaanxi is a province of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. The Wei valley, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a>, was the homeland of the Zhou and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and the seat of the Western Zhou, Qin, Western Han and Tang capitals. The north of the province, on the Loess Plateau, was the base of the Chinese Communists at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a> from 1935 to 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaanxi.jpg" alt="Shaanxi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (Cat's diary) 猫猫的日记本 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qianling_Mausoleum_25_2013-08.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kuomintang collapsed in 1927, the party built rural base areas, proclaimed the Chinese Soviet Republic in Jiangxi in 1931 and, driven out by Nationalist encirclement campaigns, made the Long March of 1934–35 to northern <b>Shaanxi</b>, where Yan’an became its base, during which Mao Zedong rose to the leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wujie invaded <b>Shaanxi</b> in 841; on 13 February 843 a Tang army under Shi Xiong killed 10,000 Uyghurs at 'Kill the Barbarians' Mountain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Zhou were a people of the Wei River valley in modern <b>Shaanxi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Many temples in northern China reopened because their gods were said to reclaim them: a Chenghuang Temple in Yulin, <b>Shaanxi</b>, used as a granary in the Cultural Revolution, was restored in the 1980s after the seeds stored in it were always found to have rotted.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>First centred on Henan and Shanxi, it later spread to <b>Shaanxi</b> and Hubei.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Cong tubes, bi discs and other jades like those of Liangzhu have been found in the Longshan culture of Shandong, at Taosi in Shanxi, in the Qijia culture and at many sites in northern <b>Shaanxi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
